Q: Does `ext4` use `i_generation` inside `name_to_handle_at`? A: <h2>Prologue</h2>
<p>Even though I'm mostly interested in <code>ext</code>-based file-systems <em>(specifically <code>ext4</code>)</em>, I'll try to answer this as broadly as I can *(took me an hour of looking into the linux-kernel &amp; [<strong>disclamer</strong>] a few prompts to grasp how precisely works... <strong>Therefore,</strong> I decided to share it in case it might help others in the future as well. <strong>+</strong> because I also wanted a place to gather all the information I collected.</p>
<h2>Answer</h2>
<p><strong>Yes</strong>, a curated\abstract idea of how <code>name_to_handle_at</code> actually does that internally, goes like this:</p>
<ol>
<li><code>name_to_handle_at</code> syscall <em>(user-space entry point)</em></li>
<li><code>do_sys_name_to_handle()</code> in <code>fs/fhandle.c</code> <em>(syscall handler)</em></li>
<li><code>exportfs_encode_fh()</code> in <code>fs/exportfs/expfs.c</code> <em>(generic encoding dispatcher)</em></li>
<li><code>exportfs_encode_inode_fh()</code> in <code>fs/exportfs/expfs.c</code> <em>(gets the filesystem-specific operations)</em></li>
<li><code>nop-&gt;encode_fh(...)</code> <em>(filesystem-specific callback, e.g., <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/3036cd0d3328220a1858b1ab390be8b562774e8a/fs/libfs.c#L1435-L1474" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>generic_encode_ino32_fh</code></a> for <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/3036cd0d3328220a1858b1ab390be8b562774e8a/fs/ext4/super.c#L1673-L1679" rel="nofollow noreferrer">ext4</a>)</em></li>
<li>File handle populated with <code>i_generation</code>, <code>i_ino</code>, and parent info</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Practicly</strong> you have at <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/fs/fhandle.c" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>linux/fs/fhandle.c</code></a> -&gt; <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/fs/fhandle.c#L115-L168" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>name_to_handle_at</code></a> calling <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/3036cd0d3328220a1858b1ab390be8b562774e8a/fs/fhandle.c#L18-L112" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>do_sys_name_to_handle</code></a> which in turn calls <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/fs/exportfs/expfs.c" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>linux/fs/exportfs/expfs.c</code></a> -&gt; <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/3036cd0d3328220a1858b1ab390be8b562774e8a/fs/exportfs/expfs.c#L415-L436" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>exportfs_encode_fh</code></a> calling <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/3036cd0d3328220a1858b1ab390be8b562774e8a/fs/exportfs/expfs.c#L381-L404" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>exportfs_encode_inode_fh</code></a> where <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/3036cd0d3328220a1858b1ab390be8b562774e8a/fs/exportfs/expfs.c#L384" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>const struct export_operations *nop = inode-&gt;i_sb-&gt;s_export_op;</code></a> gets the apropriate operations <em>(for the file-system in which the inode belongs to [e.g. <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/3036cd0d3328220a1858b1ab390be8b562774e8a/fs/libfs.c#L1435-L1474" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>generic_encode_ino32_fh</code></a> for <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/3036cd0d3328220a1858b1ab390be8b562774e8a/fs/ext4/super.c#L1673-L1679" rel="nofollow noreferrer">ext4</a>])</em> finally used at <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/3036cd0d3328220a1858b1ab390be8b562774e8a/fs/exportfs/expfs.c#L393" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>nop-&gt;encode_fh(...)</code></a> to populate the file-handle with <code>i_generation</code> + etc.</p>
<p><em>(for file systems that don't belong to the &quot;generic encoding dispatcher&quot; you may look for <a href="https://github.com/search?q=repo%3Atorvalds%2Flinux+_encode_fh&amp;type=code" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>..._encode_fh</code></a>) (Now if you ask me how or where inodes end up with <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/3036cd0d3328220a1858b1ab390be8b562774e8a/fs/exportfs/expfs.c#L384" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>i_sb-&gt;s_export_op</code></a> idk... haven't looked that deep)</em></p>
<h2>Alternative how-to</h2>
<p><strong>The correct way</strong> to acquire a file handle <strong>can be found in <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/open_by_handle_at.2.html#EXAMPLES" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>man name_to_handle_at</code></a></strong>.</p>
<p><strong>However,</strong> if for any reason you want something potentially faster and specific to an ext4 system (or other filesystems using the &quot;generic encoding&quot;), you can bypass the higher-level <code>name_to_handle_at</code> interface and use something like the following approach:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>/*
 WARNING: This is filesystem-specific and non-portable: 
 it only reconstructs a handle that may match the current
 inode-generation encoding used by a given filesystem 
 (for example ext4). The byte layout, endianness, and 
 export format are not guaranteed, and the result is not
 a general replacement for name_to_handle_at() or 
 guaranteed to always work with open_by_handle_at().

 I made this example just to prove to myself
 that I understood corectly how ext4 specifically is handled
 by the linux-kernel.**Please,** do your research before you 
 even consider using it, because my naive brain might have 
 been missing crucial stuffs here.
*/


#define _GNU_SOURCE
#include &lt;sys/ioctl.h&gt;
#include &lt;linux/fs.h&gt;
#include &lt;sys/stat.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;fcntl.h&gt;


int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    if (argc != 2)
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);

    int fd = open(argv[1], O_RDONLY);
    __u32 generation=0;

    if (ioctl(fd, FS_IOC_GETVERSION, &amp;generation))
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);

    struct statx stx; // plain stat might be faster? idk
    statx(fd, &quot;&quot;, AT_EMPTY_PATH,  STATX_INO, &amp;stx);

    // WARNING: Endianness + later use with open_by_handle_at()
    __u64 handle_bytes = ((__u64)generation &lt;&lt; 32) | (__u32)stx.stx_ino;

    printf(&quot;8 1   &quot;); // length + type
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 8; i++)
        printf(&quot; %02x&quot;, ((unsigned char*)&amp;handle_bytes)[i]);
    printf(&quot;\n&quot;);

    // then you may use the printed handle with 
    // the `t_open_by_handle_at.c`-example
    // at `man name_to_handle_at`
}
</code></pre>
<h2>Extra References</h2>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20052912/">How do I get the generation number of an inode in Linux?</a></li>
<li><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/18742032/">Why do we need to change files i_generation number?</a></li>
</ul>

Q: Speeding up SPSC Queue on C++ A: <p>If I read your benchmark correctly, you are hammering the CPU when failing to write a message:</p>
<pre><code>...
while (!messages_queue_.write(msg)) {
            }
...
</code></pre>
<p>I think you are doing the same on the consumer side in your benchmark. This takes CPU time from the scheduler (and other threads/processes) doing absolutely nothing. In my experience you may get better performance by yiedling to the OS scheduler when the FIFO users can't read or write. E.g.:</p>
<pre><code>
while (!messages_queue_.write(msg)) 
    {
    std::this_thread::yield();
    }
</code></pre>
